  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   The current approach relies heavily on local search procedures (alternating LP, coordinate descent, focusing on active constraints) that may easily get trapped in local optima. The central idea is to use a multi-phase optimization strategy that escalates the problem by increasing resolution (grid points) to escape local minima, combining it with span-optimized rank-1 bounds and minimal convex relaxations for each phase.\n\nThe algorithm will be designed to:\n   - Work with variable resolution (20, 40, 80, 160, 320, 640, 1280, 2560 grid points)\n   - Employ span-optimized rank-1 bounds verified via Kalantari's algorithm\n   - Incrementally scale through resolutions while storing the best solution\n   - Use three distinct optimization procedures for escalating resolution: conservative multi-phase optimizer, aggressive multi-resolution exploration, and final local polishing\n\nThe rationale for resolution escalation stems from the observation that current grid-based local search methods frequently converge to suboptimal solutions when confined to intermediate resolutions. Higher resolution allows escaping local extrema.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nObjective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nSubject to:\n   0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x \u2208 [0,2]\n   \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1\n\n   With resolution n = n_points, we discretize [0,2] into points x_i = i\u00b7\u0394x, with \u0394x = 2.0 / n.\n\nDiscrete form:\n   Let h \u2208 [0,1]^{n}, sum(h) = n/2 exactly (for discrete integral without scaling)\n   C_5 = dx * max_k corr_k(h), where corr_k(h) = \u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] \u00b7 (1 - h[i+k]) for valid indices.\n\nOptimization procedure:\n\nPhase A: Coordinate Descent with Active Constraints\n   For a given grid point set size n:\n   Let A = set of active lags found by:\n      for each lag k, let S_k(h) = \u2211_{i} h[i](1-h[i+k])\n   Then find N_lags = min(n, n_qual) lags with S_k(h) closest to 1 (the target)\n\n   For each grid point i, form quadratic constraint:\n      \u2211_{active_k} [a_k h[i] + b_k(1 - h[i])] \u2264 1\n   Where a_k = count_k (slope) and b_k adjusted.\n\n   Solve with CVX for field values between prior and boundary score to respect feasible region.\n\nPhase B: GridRefinement(n)\n   Starting from best solution to date (with lower resolution):\n   - If resolution n, then initialize h with scaled parent solution\n   - Apply Procedure A in three nested; reset-best solutions\n   - Use Kalantari bounds to generate random perturbations conforming to [0,1]^n and integral=1\n   - Use multi-stage optimization: LP formulation, then gradient-based refinement\n\nPhase C: LocalPolish(grid_size)\n   Finest grid level; perform coordinate descent followed by focused LP on 20-50% of active constraints; use random sampling to diversify\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\n   a. Check budget & validation\n\n   b. Initialize with 400-point parent solution if provided\n\n   c. Loop over resolutions (n in [16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048]) if time remains:\n\n      i.  Multi-Phase Optimizer:\n         1. if optimizer_type == 'intensive', run full Optimization A\n         2. elif optimizer_type == 'aggressive', run Optimization B\n         3. store best solution found at this resolution\n\n      ii. Downsample final solution to coarse resolution for grid comparison\n\n   d. LocalPolish on finest resolution found before budget exhaustion\n\n   e. Comparison between resolution results and return best possible\n\n   To handle multi-resolution:\n\n   - New grid implementation validates parent through yield_switch (budget ratio)\n   - Tracks best solution ever found during any resolution iteration\n   - Intervals for phase termination driven by elapsed time (increased by factor 2 with each resolution)\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\n   Procedure A (intensive) details:\n\n      \u2022 Precompute correlation vector for all valid lags (with DiscreteConvolution using real time for grid)\n      \u2022 Use tinyMPS formula for vectorized objective: \u2211_{active_k} (h[i] \u00b7 (1-h[i+k])) <= 1 for all i\n      \u2022 Variables as single vector, minimize trivial objective (not C_5) but maximize sum of bounds\n      \u2022 Bounds as Kalantari randomized bounds around nominal 0.5 field values scaled to total sum\n\n   Procedure B (aggressive) details:\n\n      \u2022 Random sampling of grid space bounded by 0.0-1.0 with sample size = min(round(500/n)^2, current_budget)\n      \u2022 Not expensive, only applies if intensive phase didn't catch current record\n      \u2022 Uses only 5 stages covering increasing fraction of grid to find improvements faster\n\n   Procedure LocalPolish details:\n\n      \u2022 Pure coordinate descent with OPT: strict 20% of active lags only, 20,000 max passes\n      \u2022 Louvain-inspired restarts after 10 passes with noise corruption (\u03c3=0.01)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n   Total budget: 1300 seconds\n\n   Resolution n=4 is affordable (eval = 16\u00b7scale)\n               provides robust fallback\n\n   The intensive procedure has:\n      1. CVX solve: O(n) per grid point, so O(n^2) operations\n      2. Active lags selection: O(n^2) elements, so O(n^2) eval\n\n   Estimated runtime for n=2048: 600 seconds (in CVX with multiple solvers)\n\n   Workaround for CVX-DPP complexity: precompute constraint handling using sparse matrix with nc=20, nlags~80\n\n   Budget safeguards: if (budget use > 0.9 budget_s), proactive exit.\n\n6. Validation and Output Format\n\n   For each candidate solution:\n     - Run exact correlation evaluation on the top nth row\n     - Verify sum(h)\u22481 (tol 1e-8)\n     - Check 0<=h[i]<=1 for all i\n\n   Return format: (h_values, C_5_lower, grid_size)\n\n   cross_validate:\n\n     finalSolution(400) = main.optimizer(parentSolution)\n     newSolution = run_intensive(optimizer, finalSolution) if (timelimit_remaining > 60)\n\n   Outcome is one of: \n     { newSolutionC, 2040, current_best }",
